# Design: the emitter, written in form. The page spec/core-operations.md is the authority (entries 102, 112 and 128).

n: 5.18
name: the-emitter
kind: hub
is: The emitter written in form: a module of named definitions that compiles definitions over numbers and nodes, with what they call, to one machine-code leaf. Its code answers a number or a node, or declines, is held to the walker's outcome by two programs, and for an entry is a faster form of the entry's own binding.
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.18.1
name: an-emitter-in-form
kind: decision
is: Entry 128, compile, has no code in the bootstrap; form/compile.aarch64.ck binds it to an emitter written in form, a layer of named definitions. One pass over a recipe's body gathers each node's instructions as bytes and packs them into one code leaf. It knows numbers, let, tests joined by if, control (5.18.9), searches (5.18.11, 5.18.12), calls among definitions (5.18.5) and, by asking the bootstrap, the parts of a node and the building of one (5.18.6). For any other node it emits a byte that pack refuses, so a recipe is compiled whole or stays on the walker.
status: inferred
from: 5.10.4 5.14.19 5.14.15 7.13 7.21
src: 2.30.6
conf: mid
why: The emitter is to be a program in the language (5.10.4), and the walker already gives it what it needs: a node's parts, arithmetic, all and pack. Dividing by 256 is done by reading the bytes of a product, since no operation works on bits.
flip: Emitted code calls the bootstrap's operations on values (5.18.6) and not the walker. Per-node hand-back, as 5.10.4 describes, needs that; until then a recipe with one node that searches is not compiled at all.
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.18.2
name: code-and-walker-are-held-equal-by-a-program
kind: observation
is: Observed on 2026-11-26. ground/code-or-walker.ck runs twenty-nine recipes on the walker and again after asking for their code and holds the outcomes equal: nested lets, large and negative integers, division by zero and of the most negative number, tests with a nothing inside, and three the emitter refuses. The emitter was first written again from one recipe of bare numbers into named definitions and gave byte for byte the same code for seven recipes; then it was extended. Six faults planted in it each turned the program red.
status: observed
src: 2.30.6 2.30.7
limit: The program's first version saw none of the planted faults: its helper was a bound entry, which runs on the bindings beneath its own and so never saw the code. The numbers are 64-bit integers only. One machine.
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.18.3
name: bound-says-what-runs-an-entry
kind: decision
is: Entry 102, bound, answers the newest binding of an entry or a recipe as the top of the program sees it, as a node: the entry, its implementation, and whether that runs on its own binding. A program can read what is bound; the emitter takes an entry's recipe from it and compiles a call by the recipe's own name only where that name means the recipe itself, which is so for a binding made in a layer.
status: inferred
from: 5.14.19 5.18.1 7.13
src: 2.30.7 2.30.6
conf: mid
why: Without it a program could bind and release but not look, and the emitter had to be handed the recipe again.
flip: It answers the top's view and not the asker's layer. An implementation asking about its own entry gets itself.
limit: With a second entry it answers as that entry's implementation sees the first.
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.18.4
name: code-is-a-faster-form-of-the-binding
kind: decision
is: Entry 112, fast, gives the newest binding of an entry a code leaf as its faster form: tried first wherever that binding runs, the recipe running when it declines. The binding stays where it is, so the other definitions of its layer see the code. It steps aside while something bound from outside sits on a bootstrap operation or on an entry the program defines. Observed on 2026-11-26: a definition of the same layer ran on the code; with a counter on add from outside every addition was seen, and without it the code ran again.
status: inferred
from: 5.18.1 5.18.3 5.17.1 5.14.19 7.21
src: 2.30.7 2.30.6
conf: mid
why: Code bound above a recipe is unseen by everything bound before it, which is most of a program written as a layer of definitions. Code is not another meaning of the entry; it is the same meaning in another form, so it belongs with the binding it is a form of.
flip: A form set on a binding is not undone by a fail. Watching from outside turns every faster form off, not only those that replaced what is watched.
limit: Witnessed in ground/code-or-walker.ck, turned red by two planted faults.
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.18.5
name: definitions-compiled-together
kind: observation
is: Observed on 2026-11-26. The emitter compiles an entry with every definition it calls into one leaf behind a table of branches, so the leaf lies wherever it is put. A call in the last position is a jump when nothing has overflowed. Two definitions that call each other, and a loop calling a third, ended as on the walker; that loop ran thirty million rounds in 0.4 s. Beneath a faster form that declined, faster forms are not tried again: a recursion deeper than the machine stack was answered by the walker.
status: observed
src: 2.30.6 2.30.7
limit: No backtracking. Before the rule about declining, such a recursion tried its code again at every step and did not end in two minutes. Two more faults planted in the emitter, a wrong slot and a jump that ignores an overflow, each turned ground/code-or-walker.ck red.
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.18.6
name: code-asks-the-bootstrap
kind: decision
is: The machine's serve, at byte 128 of the region, is the address of one function of the bootstrap that does an operation on values in hand. Code calls it under the contract it is called by: a number as it is, any other value as its handle, one register saying how many and which. quote is the value itself, all the list of those that are something, node the node of an id, and any operation on values alone is itself. A fault there is the program's and does not return to the code. So code reads and builds nodes while the store stays the bootstrap's.
status: inferred
from: 5.18.1 5.14.33 5.10.9 7.21
src: 2.30.6 2.30.7
conf: mid
why: 5.10.9 asks that code be handed what it needs and hold no addresses of the store. One function for every operation keeps what code sees to one address and one contract, and what it gets is what the walker would get: the same C does both.
flip: Each read of a node is a call. Operations written out as instructions would be faster and would tie the code to the store's layout. The handle of an entry of the bootstrap's catalogue is written into the instructions, since it is the same in every run; any other node is named by its id, which the bootstrap looks up at each use.
limit: The operations on values were one stretch of the walker and are now one function that both use. Control and the calling of recipes are not served.
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.18.7
name: every-value-has-a-kind
kind: decision
is: The emitter knows for every value whether it is a number or a node. A parameter used as a number is taken as one, and the code declines a call that gives anything else; any other parameter takes anything, a number given for it being made a node on the way in. Where the other kind is wanted the bootstrap is asked for it. After an overflow, a nothing where a number was wanted or a decline, no further call is made and the code is marked declined: the bootstrap is never handed a value that means nothing.
status: inferred
from: 5.18.1 5.18.6 5.10.4
src: 2.30.6
conf: mid
why: Numbers stay in registers, where the speed is; nodes are handles, which is what the bootstrap takes. The kinds change how fast the code is and not what it answers, since each crossing is exact. A fault the code did not reach is left for the walker to find.
flip: A parameter used both ways is a number, so a call that gives it a node declines. Kinds are settled once for a definition, not for each call.
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.18.8
name: code-on-nodes-is-held-to-the-walker
kind: observation
is: Observed on 2026-11-26. ground/code-on-nodes.ck runs eighty recipes over nodes on the walker and again as code and holds the outcomes equal: reading, comparing and building, constants, a nothing and a fault wherever one can arise, definitions handing nodes to each other. It says for each whether the code answered: sixty-five did, and the fifteen that end in nothing or a fault were handed back. Counting the lens's definition as a tree, 5,711 nodes, 300 times took 0.67 s walked and 0.04 s compiled; a chain of a million pairs was built and summed in 0.13 s against 0.53 s.
status: observed
src: 2.30.6 2.30.7
limit: Twelve faults planted in the emitter and three in the bootstrap each turned it red; one, a call skipped without the decline being marked, was seen only after a case was written for it. One machine.
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.18.9
name: a-failure-is-a-quiet-mark
kind: decision
is: Emitted code knows seq, need, fail and a first over alternatives that each answer once or fail. A failure is a mark beside the mark of a decline: what follows is passed over with no call made, until the condition of an if, the next alternative of a first or a part of a row takes it. What a failed stretch collected, an overflow or a nothing, goes with it; a decline stays. Between definitions compiled together a failure passes as one; where the walker called the code it is a decline, and the walker fails in its own way.
status: inferred
from: 5.18.1 5.18.7 5.4.6
src: 2.30.6
conf: mid
why: Code runs straight on with no jump out of the middle of an expression, so one pass still writes it; calls are the only things that must not happen after a failure, and each already looks at the mark before it is made.
flip: After a failure the rest of the definition is still stepped through, doing nothing. A definition that fails where the walker called it runs twice.
limit: ground/code-without-search.ck holds thirty-six recipes to the walker's outcome; ten planted faults each turned it red, one only after a case was written where failing and answering nothing differ.
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.18.10
name: the-emitter-compiles-part-of-itself
kind: observation
is: Observed on 2026-11-26. In ground/compiles-its-own.ck the emitter, walked, compiles the 228 definitions the other modules bind: 112 give code. It then compiles its own 149, of which 95 give code, and on that code compiles the 228 again: every leaf is byte for byte what it wrote walked, in 0.5 s against 2.1 s; the program holds the equality and that the second pass took under half the first. Each of the 207 compiles the same alone in a fresh process. The reader of the print in form read the lens's 19,780 bytes in 20 ms walked and 7 ms after.
status: observed
src: 2.30.6 2.30.7
limit: At 292 definitions the count first differed between two programs, 85 and 102 against 103 alone: the root as a constant had been taken for a number, so a compiled test of the emitter's own answered no for every entry. One machine.
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.18.11
name: a-search-is-a-loop
kind: decision
is: Inside an all or a first, emitted code searches without the walker's choice points: a range is a loop, the alternatives of a choice follow one another, a need that does not hold jumps past what depends on it, and an operation with a search among its parts is done for each answer of that part. What comes after an answer is written out where the answer arises. all gathers in the bootstrap and takes a list; first keeps its first answer and sets a mark, stopped, under which loops end and no call is made. What a search keeps between answers lies in the frame.
status: inferred
from: 5.18.9 5.18.6 5.4.6 5.10.4
src: 2.30.6
conf: mid
why: Code that leaves no mark has nothing to undo, so a search needs no trail and no choice point: going back is going on to the next round of a loop. The emitter stays one pass, the continuation being a recipe it holds while it writes.
flip: What follows an answer is written again for each alternative, so wide searches make large code. A search stands only under an all or a first or as a whole definition (5.18.12), not as the condition of an if.
limit: ground/code-searches.ck holds thirty-three searches to the walker's outcome; seven planted faults each turned it red. Primes below 3,000 and pairs below 400: 79 ms walked, 2 ms compiled. One machine.
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.18.12
name: a-definition-hands-its-answers-on
kind: decision
is: A definition that answers more than once is compiled as a search of its own. Where it has an answer it calls the code of the search that called it, given to it with the caller's frame; that code does what follows in the caller and says whether to go on, and a first that has its answer says stop. So a search crosses definitions, and one may call itself while answering. What a search keeps between answers lies in its frame, since the code that takes an answer runs beneath the definition that gave it. Such a definition is compiled only in the leaf of one that searches it.
status: inferred
from: 5.18.11 5.18.9 5.4.6
src: 2.30.6
conf: mid
why: Going back into a definition for its next answer needs its state kept somewhere; calling forward with each answer keeps that state where it already is, on the machine stack, and needs no choice point. The walker takes one answer at a time, so from the walker such a definition stays walked.
flip: Each answer passes through the code of every search above it: answers from depth d of a recursion cost d calls. Handing the caller's own continuation straight down would cut that; it is not done.
limit: ground/code-hands-answers.ck holds twenty-five recipes to the walker's outcome, a walk of the lens's 5,711 nodes among them. Five planted faults turned it red; a sixth, a stop not obeyed, made an endless definition run on without end. One machine.
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.18.13
name: a-note-from-code-is-kept-back
kind: decision
is: Code may write a note. The bootstrap keeps it back in an area of its own and writes the cell when the code has answered; when the code declines, the note is dropped, since the walker runs the call again and writes it itself. held, asked by code, reads what was kept back before it reads the cell. So a definition that remembers what it computed is compiled, and a decline after a note still leaves each note written once. The emitter refuses a definition only while an operation it uses, or the step, is bound otherwise.
status: inferred
from: 5.18.6 5.18.7 5.14.34
src: 2.30.6 2.30.7
conf: mid
why: A note is what was seen of a run and no fail undoes it, so it needs no trail: keeping it back until the answer is the whole of what a decline needs. hold is undone by a fail and bind changes what runs; neither is kept back, and neither is compiled.
flip: A note is not seen by another process until the code that wrote it has answered.
limit: Witnessed in ground/code-on-nodes.ck; three faults planted in the bootstrap, a dropped note written all the same, a kept one never written and one not read back, each turned it red, two of them only after cases were written for them.
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.18.14
name: the-emitter-is-written-with-shapes-and-named-cases
kind: observation
is: Observed on 2026-11-26. The emitter was rewritten in steps, each holding that it wrote byte for byte the code it wrote before for every definition of the other modules. Its rows, its place among them and its surroundings are shapes read by name; the definition that picked a case in 655 lines picks it in 72, each case a definition of its own; every instruction has one of 55 named builders, and no raw encoding stands outside them; sequences written several times are defined once. It has 149 definitions, the longest 102 lines, and 95 of them compile where 61 of 91 did.
status: observed
src: 2.30.6 2.30.7
limit: 61 reads by a bare number are left in it, most the first or second child of a node of unknown blueprint. One machine.
at: 2026-11-26
